  stickyNoteInfos:
  - title: Vector Rotation
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: 1609.0002
      y: 1353.8
      width: 403
      height: 179
    contents: "We're creating a sine wave based on time. This \u201Ctime\u201D input
      is multiplied so that we can control the speed of the oscillation.\r\n\r\nThe
      sine wave value is then multiplied so that we can control the \u201Camplitude\u201D
      of the oscillation.\r\n\r\nThis is used in a \u201Ctransform direction\u201D
      operator. So we're rotating the direction (0,1,0) on the Z-axis.\r\n\r\nWe
      could have used a \u201CRotate 3D\u201D operator for the same result.\r\n"
    theme: 
    textSize: Small
    colorTheme: 2
  - title: Collapse
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: 1602.0002
      y: 1159.8
      width: 246
      height: 134
    contents: 'When double-clicking on an operator, you''re able to collapse it to
      its minimal form.

      This is especially useful to reduce visual noise in
      the graph.

      Try it yourself; double-click to collapse or expand an operator.

'
    theme: 
    textSize: Small
    colorTheme: 2
  - title: '03.GIZMO Visualisation:  '
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: 3218
      y: 1194
      width: 416
      height: 214
    contents: "Many blocks give you the options to either tweak the value directly
      within the block itself or by manipulating \u201CVisual Gizmos\u201D right
      in the \u201CScene View.\u201D\r\n\r\nFor this, you need to \u201Cattach\u201D
      the VFXG that you're editing to a corresponding VFX instance in your scene.\r\n\r\nOn
      the top bar of the VFXG tab, you will see a small \u201Clink\u201D icon that
      lets you link or attach the graph to the selected VFXG in the scene.\r\n\r\nWith
      the attachment made, selecting the \u201Cinitialize Context Block\u201D should
      allow you to see the \u201CBound Gizmo\u201D (if manual or record mode is on).\r\nYou
      can directly edit the Gizmo in the scene.\r\n"
    theme: 
    textSize: Small
    colorTheme: 2
  - title: '01.Bounds:'
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: 2736
      y: 664
      width: 234
      height: 327
    contents: "The system is only visible if the bounding box is in the \u201Ccamera
      frustum.\u201D\r\nYou can set your bounds in three ways:\r\n\r\nManual: \r\nLets
      you set the Bound Size and Center manually.\r\n\r\nRecorded:\r\nWhen set to
      record, users can open \u201CVFX Control\u201D and \u201CRecord\u201D their
      bound computation. When done, you can apply them to the system.\r\n\r\nAutomatic:\r\nBounds
      will be computed each frame. This can be needed for dynamic VFX or when iterating
      on a VFX. Be aware that this consumes resources, and it\u2019s better to use
      the manual or recorded mode when possible. \r\n"
    theme: 
    textSize: Small
    colorTheme: 2
  - title: '02.Bounds experiment:'
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: 3040
      y: 774
      width: 266
      height: 207
    contents: 'Culling a system is important for performance, and that''s why bound''s
      role is crucial.



      In this example, the bounds have been set to manual
      and have been misplaced on purpose.



      You should see the particles disappear
      when focusing the camera on the VFX origin.



      Try the different bound
      modes and adjust the bounds so that the particles don''t disappear.

'
    theme: 
    textSize: Small
    colorTheme: 2
  - title: BOUNDS
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: 2786
      y: 330
      width: 375
      height: 103
    contents: 
    theme: 
    textSize: Huge
    colorTheme: 2
  categories: []
  uiBounds:
    serializedVersion: 2
    x: 1568
    y: 270
    width: 2092
    height: 2354
